---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../site.config.js';
---

<Page
  title="Source policy"
  eyebrow="Editorial"
  lede="What we cite, how we rank it, and what we will not cite at any tier."
  updated="29 August 2026"
>
  <p>
    Not all sources carry the same weight, and a profile that treats them as if they did is misleading even when
    every individual link works. Every source cited on this site is labelled with a tier, shown next to the citation
    on the profile itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Tier 1 — strong</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Government departments, regulators and public registries</li>
    <li>Court and other public records</li>
    <li>Major national newspapers and broadcasters</li>
    <li>Academic publications and established publishers</li>
    <li>Official awarding and accrediting bodies, for their own awards</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tier 2 — good</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Established specialist and industry publications</li>
    <li>Local newspapers with an identifiable editorial staff</li>
    <li>Professional associations and sector bodies</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tier 3 — supporting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Smaller specialist outlets</li>
    <li>Self-published expert sources, where the author is identifiable and the subject is within their expertise</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tier 3 sources can support detail, but a profile is not published on tier 3 evidence alone.
  </p>

  <h2>First-party</h2>
  <p>
    A subject's own website, filings, press releases and social accounts. These are cited for what a subject says
    about itself — a founding year, a stated mission, a programme list — and are marked
    <span class="fp">first-party</span> wherever they appear. They are never presented as independent
    confirmation of the same claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Not cited</h2>
  <p>These are excluded regardless of what they happen to say:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Business and school directories that aggregate unverified data</li>
    <li>Scraped or mirrored sites</li>
    <li>AI-generated content farms</li>
    <li>Anonymous blogs and SEO spam</li>
    <li>Content the subject paid to place, where that is detectable</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Directory listings are sometimes useful as a research lead — a phone number to check, a detail to look for in a
    better source — but they do not appear in a citation list.
  </p>

  <h2>Domains that mislead</h2>
  <p>
    A trusted domain does not make a page trustworthy. Some publishers host paid or user-submitted material on the
    same domain as their journalism, and the URL gives no warning. Known cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Forbes Councils</strong> (<code>councils.forbes.com</code>, and "Forbes Councils Member" bylines) are
      paid contributor posts, not Forbes journalism. Staff-written Forbes articles are tier 1; council posts are
      not cited at any tier.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Press releases carried on news domains</strong> — wire reproductions and sponsored posts — are
      first-party material wherever they appear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Encyclopedias and wikis</strong>, including Wikipedia, are not cited. Where they are useful, it is as
      a pointer to a primary source, which we then read ourselves.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Check the byline and the page furniture, not the domain.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule the site is built on</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Every source cited has been opened and read against the claim it supports.</strong> A link that exists is
    not evidence that it says what a draft claims it says. This check has already caught fabricated citations in our
    own drafts, and it is a required step before any profile is published.
  </p>
  <p>
    A profile that cannot muster at least one tier 1 or tier 2 source is not published. This is enforced by the site's
    build: a profile whose sources are all first-party or tier 3 fails the build rather than going live.
  </p>

  <p style="font-size:0.95rem; color:var(--ink-soft)">
    Questions about a specific citation: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.correctionsEmail}`}>{SITE.correctionsEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
